black lines matter demonstrations over  the summer at times were pretty intense  protesters outraged over the killing of  george floyd an unarmed black man killed  in police custody  all this week we're examining what  changes metro police have made and what  still needs work  bianca holman shares how black officers  are straddling the line  black police officers are in the middle  they serve the valley in uniform but are  frustrated with the disproportionate  number of unarmed black people  killed by police black officers say they  faced  challenges on the job and in the  community when it comes to their race  however the black police officers  association bpoa  is a way they're breaking down barriers  to confront racism  in recent years the organization hosted  community events to help  build relationships and trust president  regina coward-holman says  if metro's mission is to serve people in  need then they must be held accountable  building bridges  and being the conscience of the justice  system we see something that's wrong  we have to found the bill we have to  speak up we can no longer just sit back  like these things didn't happen  tonight at 11 we'll look at the advisory  council that's in place to provide  feedback to the sheriff  how much of metro staff is  african-american and those officers  ideas on how to improve the force  reporting bianca holman 8 news now
 

